# BCIP Frontend-First Implementation Plan

**Version:** 1.1
**Corresponding SRS:** BCIP SRS v1.3
**Purpose:** Guide an AI coding assistant (Claude Code) to build BCIP's **frontend first, against a mocked API contract**, then build the real Django/DRF/PostgreSQL/blockchain backend and swap the mocks out one endpoint at a time.

**Changelog from v1.0:** Added a binding "UI Development Standards" section (single source of truth for color/tokens, mandatory component reuse, no assumed/arbitrary colors, status signaling beyond color alone, consistent layout/spacing) that governs every Part A phase. Phase 0 now explicitly establishes the token file, status/icon definitions, and shared page-shell layouts. Phases 2–5 each gained a "UI Standards check" line in their completion criteria. Phase 6 is now also a formal, checklist-driven UI Standards audit gate before Part B begins.

---

## Why Frontend-First, and the Risk It Introduces

Building the UI first lets you validate the user experience, screen flow, and data shape early, without waiting on Docker, PostgreSQL, Django, or a deployed smart contract. The trade-off is **contract drift**: if the frontend invents response shapes as it goes, the backend built later may not match, forcing rework on one side or the other.

This plan manages that risk with one rule: **Phase 1 freezes a written API contract before any UI component is built.** Every frontend phase codes against that contract via a mock service layer. Every backend phase's job is to make the real API match the same contract exactly — not to redesign it. If the backend genuinely needs to deviate from the contract, the contract document is updated first and the frontend's mock/types are updated to match, in a dedicated small task, before backend work continues.

---

## How to Use This Plan

1. Open this file and the SRS (v1.3) in your Claude Code session.
2. Work through **Part A (Frontend)** fully before starting **Part B (Backend)**, except Phase 1 (API Contract), which both parts depend on and should never be skipped or rushed.
3. In Part B, each phase begins with an explicit "Frontend Integration" step: point the existing UI at the real endpoint and delete the corresponding mock. Do not leave both a mock and a real implementation active for the same endpoint past the end of that phase.
4. Verify each phase's "Phase Completion Criteria" before moving on.
5. **Every task in Part A is additionally governed by the UI Development Standards below.** These are not a separate phase — they are binding rules applied continuously, checked at the end of every frontend phase alongside that phase's own completion criteria.

---

## UI Development Standards (Binding — Applies to Every Frontend Phase)

These rules exist to prevent the most common way an AI-assisted build turns unprofessional: each phase silently reinventing its own button, its own shade of blue, its own spacing scale, because it was built in isolation from the phases before it. The rule is not "make it look nice" — it's **"there is exactly one source of truth for every visual decision, and every phase must use it, not invent a new one."**

### 1. Single Source of Truth for Color — No Assumed Colors

- All color values live in **one place**: the design tokens defined in Phase 0 (e.g., a Tailwind config, CSS variables file, or theme object). No component, in any phase, may contain a raw hex/RGB value or an arbitrary Tailwind color class (`text-blue-500`, `bg-[#3b82f6]`, etc.) — it must reference a named token (`text-primary`, `bg-surface`, `text-danger`).
- **Claude must not invent a color palette from assumption or default framework colors.** Before Phase 0 builds any token file, either (a) the user supplies brand colors/a reference, or (b) Claude asks a single clarifying question to establish a minimal palette (primary, neutral scale, and semantic success/warning/danger/info) rather than silently picking "professional-looking blue" and moving on.
- Status-driven colors are the highest-risk spot for drift, since the same five-state enum (`PENDING`/`VALID`/`EXPIRED`/`REVOKED`/`FAILED`) and four verification outcomes (`VALID`/`EXPIRED`/`REVOKED`/`TAMPERED`) get rendered in at least three different screens (dashboard table, certificate detail, public verification result) across three different phases. Define the color **and** the non-color signal (icon/label — see Rule 3) for each status **once**, in the Phase 0 token/component layer, and have every later phase import it. A later phase is never allowed to pick its own color for `REVOKED` because "it looked fine there."
- Any new semantic color need discovered mid-plan (e.g., a "stale-PENDING" warning state introduced in Phase 4) must be added to the central token file first, then consumed — never hardcoded locally "for now."

### 2. Component Reuse — No Duplication

- Before writing any new UI element, check the shared component set from Phase 0 (Button, Input, Card, Badge/StatusPill, Table, Modal, Toast) and later additions to it. If something close already exists, extend it with a prop rather than creating a near-duplicate component or copy-pasting markup with small tweaks.
- If a genuinely new reusable pattern emerges in a later phase (e.g., a multi-step form wizard for password reset in Phase 3), it is built **once**, added to the shared component set, and reused — the 3-step password-reset wizard and any future multi-step flow use the same wizard component, not two independent implementations.
- Forms across the app (register, login, reset password, certificate creation) share one form-field/label/error-message pattern, not per-screen variations.
- Before marking any phase complete, scan the phase's new components for near-duplicate JSX/markup against the existing shared set. If two components are >80% structurally identical, they must be merged or one must be built on top of the other via composition/props.

### 3. Status and State Signaling — Never Color Alone

- Every status or outcome (certificate status, verification result, form validation state) is communicated through **at least two channels** — color plus one of: icon, text label, or shape/border — so the UI remains professional and accessible, and so a screenshot in grayscale still communicates meaning. This was already required by Phase 6's accessibility pass; it is now a standing rule applied from Phase 2 onward, not a fix bolted on at the end.

### 4. Consistency of Layout, Spacing, and Typography

- Use the spacing scale and type scale defined in Phase 0 exclusively — no one-off `margin: 13px` or arbitrary font sizes. If the scale is missing a needed value, add it to the token file, don't work around it locally.
- Page-level layout (header/nav placement, content max-width, padding rhythm) is defined once in a shared layout component/wrapper and reused by every authenticated screen (dashboard, detail, creation) and, separately, once for the public-facing screens (verification, landing). A later phase does not introduce its own page shell.

### 5. Enforcement — This Is Part of "Done," Not a Nice-to-Have

- Add "UI Standards check" as a standing line item in every Part A phase's Phase Completion Criteria (already reflected below): no raw color values, no near-duplicate components, every status shown with a non-color signal, and no page-level layout reinvention.
- If any of these are violated while building a phase, the fix belongs in the shared token/component layer, not as a local override — even if that means briefly stepping back into Phase 0's files while nominally working on a later phase.

