## Phase 1 – API Contract & Mock Data Layer (the phase that de-risks everything else)

**Goal:** A single, written, versioned API contract covering every endpoint the frontend will call, plus a mock service implementation that returns realistic fixture data matching that contract exactly. This is the artifact that both Part A and Part B build against.

**Tasks:**
- Write `API_CONTRACT.md` (or equivalent) documenting, for every endpoint in SRS §3 and the Implementation Plan's backend phases: method, path, request body shape, response body shape (success and error), and status codes. Base this directly on the SRS endpoint list — do not invent new endpoints or field names not implied by the SRS's FR-IDs and data model (§6).
- Explicitly include the 5-state certificate status enum (`PENDING`/`VALID`/`EXPIRED`/`REVOKED`/`FAILED`, FR-4.3), the token strategy shape (access token in body, refresh token as cookie — the mock layer can simulate the cookie via an in-memory flag), and the verification result shape (`VALID`/`EXPIRED`/`REVOKED`/`TAMPERED`/`NOT_FOUND`, §7.6).
- Generate matching TypeScript types/interfaces from the contract, used by every component built afterward — this is what makes "swap the mock for the real call" a mechanical, low-risk operation later.
- Build a mock service layer (e.g., MSW — Mock Service Worker, or a simple fetch-intercepting module) implementing every endpoint from the contract against an in-memory fixture dataset: a handful of organizations, certificates in each of the five statuses, and at least one certificate whose mock verification result is `TAMPERED` (so Phase 2's UI has something to render for the worst-case path, not just the happy path).
- Simulate realistic latency and occasional error responses (e.g., a 10% chance of a 500 on one endpoint, toggleable) in the mock layer, so later UI work has to handle loading/error states rather than assuming everything always succeeds instantly.

**SRS Requirements Addressed:** All of §3 and §6 (as a documentation/typing exercise, not implementation)
**Phase Completion Criteria:**
- `API_CONTRACT.md` exists and covers every endpoint referenced anywhere else in this plan and in the SRS.
- TypeScript types exist for every request/response shape in the contract.
- The mock service layer responds correctly to a manual smoke test of at least one call per resource (auth, certificates, public verification).
- Fixture data includes at least one certificate in each of the five statuses, plus one that mock-verifies as `TAMPERED`.

